"Driving Privilege Reciprocity | Department of Public Safety (https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/driving-privilege-reciprocity)\nciteturn16view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 50\nL0: Search Form Search\nL1: \nL2: [Input: What can we help you find?]\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Search]\nL5: \nL6: [Input] [Input]\nL7: \nL8: ## Change Text Size\nL9: \nL10: ^{-}A A ^{+}A\nL11: \nL12: ## Change Display Setting\nL13: \nL14: cite0†Default cite1†Text Only L15: \nL16: # Driving Privilege Reciprocity\nL17: \nL18: September 23, 2020\nL19: ###\nL20: \nL21: Driving privilege reciprocity allows a person to use a valid, unexpired foreign license to operate a motor vehicle in Texas for up to one year or until a person becomes a Texas resident, whichever date is sooner. Once a person becomes a new Texas resident, they must apply for a Texas license within 90 days to continue to drive legally. For more information, please review the cite2†Moving to Texas page.\nL22: Noncommercial licenses:\nL23: The state’s recognition of driving privilege reciprocity is governed by the following international agreements coordinated and signed by the federal government. To see a list of countries for each agreement, click the link provided.\nL24: \nL25: \nL26:   * cite3†1943 Inter-American Convention on the Regulation of Inter-American Automotive Traffic (PDF)†www.oas.org L27: \nL28:   * cite4†North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) agreement†www.nato.int and cite5†countries†www.nato.int L29:   * cite6†1949 Geneva Convention on Road Traffic (PDF)†treaties.un.org L30: \nL31:   * Per U.S. State Department consideration: Armenia, Azerbaijan, Belarus, Estonia, Kazakhstan, Latvia, Lithuania, Moldova, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, Ukraine, Uzbekistan, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Macedonia, Serbia, Slovenia, and Yugoslavia.\nL32: #### General Provisions of International Agreements:\nL33:   * Reciprocal privileges are limited to persons ages 18 to 75 for up to one year from the date of entry into the United States.\nL34:   * Reciprocal privileges are limited to private vehicles.\nL35:   * Every vehicle must have a registration certificate issued in accordance with the laws of the country of residence identifying the vehicle and owner and be displayed on the rear of the vehicle or on a plate attached to the rear.\nL36:   * Every vehicle must have an oval sign or plaque displayed on the rear of the vehicle to indicate the country of origin.\nL37:   * Every driver must have a valid driver permit, license, or international permit issued by an authorized authority and be translated into English.\nL38: Note: These are general provisions only. For the complete text of an agreement, click the links provided.\nL39: \nL40: Commercial licenses:\nL41: \nL42: Only commercial driver licenses from Canada and Mexico are recognized by the U.S. Secretary of Transportation as having full reciprocity for driving privileges in the state of Texas.\nL43: ### Follow DPS\nL44: \nL45: ### Keep Texas Safe\nL46: \nL47: cite7†Report Suspicious Activity†iwatchtx.org L48: \nL49: © 2026 Texas Department of Public Safety. PDF files require cite8†Adobe Reader†get.adobe.com or compatible.\n"
